                                    // If I set grid[idx(x,y)] = 0 (empty), then when subsequent iterations process neighbors to left/right in same row, they see empty space below them at y+1? No, that's diagonal neighbor. The sand moved down-left so it is now at x-1 on lower row y+1. Since we already processed rows > y, this new sand won't be moved again in this tick (Good). But what about the cell `(x,y)` becoming empty? It was just vacated by us; no issue.
                                    // Note: If I iterate L->R and move from x to x-1, that is moving LEFT into a column we might have ALREADY visited in this row loop! That would cause issues because that neighbor's cell `(x-1,y)` or its diagonal check logic was already run? 
                                    // Actually if we moved sand diagonally DOWN-LEFT (to x-1 on y+1), the target is below. The source is at `y`. If order L->R, when processing `x`, column `x-1` in same row has been visited earlier! But that's fine because moving to a lower cell `(x-1,y+1)` doesn't affect state of `(x-1,y)`.
                                    // Wait: Does it? The logic for sand at `(x,y)` looks below-left. If I swap or move into diagonal, the destination is in row `y+1` (already processed). So that's fine.
                                } else if (canMoveR) {
